From 263d61ef6fd6882eb6d13accd2c438c6194e0e5e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: qiyinxi <qiyinxi@foxmail.com>
Date: Tue, 6 Oct 2026 04:39:26 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 43/65] media/cuda: return textures of undelivered frames to
 the pool

MediaCudaVideoHelper::deInit() cleared mSavedDecodedFrames without
returning their textures. In GPU texture mode every saved frame owns a
Y/UV texture pair taken from MediaTexturePool. Once the frame is
dropped nothing refers to the pair any more, and the pool's
cleanUpTextures() only deletes pairs that are back in the pool, so the
textures stay allocated until the GL share group goes away.

The list is usually empty when deInit() runs. The H.264, HEVC and
VP8/VP9 decoders call fetchAllFrames() after every decode and flush,
which moves the frames into their MediaSnapshotHelper, and the H.264
and HEVC decoders hand those textures back to the pool when they are
reset or destroyed. Frames are left in the list only if the helper is
destroyed inside a decode call, before fetchAllFrames() has run. The
H.264 and HEVC decoders do that during their trial period: they check
good() after every packet and fall back to FFmpeg once it turns false.
HandleVideoSequence() makes it false when a new sequence has a format
or size the GPU cannot decode, and any picture that the parser
displays in that same cuvidParseVideoData() call is still in the list
when try_decode() destroys the helper.

Give the textures back to the pool before clearing the list; the pool
frees them in its next cleanUpTextures() (on the next reset or when the
decoder is destroyed).

Test: 150 CUDA decode sessions killed mid-stream: GPU local usage and
      whole-card usage stay flat, as they do without this change. In
      about 50 more sessions killed during unpaced decode a debugger
      counted 110 deInit() calls, none of which found a frame in the
      list. The new loop was reviewed, not exercised: a stream that
      switches to a format the GPU cannot decode has not been tried.

Assisted-by: Claude Code:Claude-Opus-5.5
Change-Id: Id27d72337f748f107b76f51c414260220f3fe7ee
---
 .../emulation/MediaCudaVideoHelper.cpp        | 19 ++++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 18 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/android/emu/media/src/android/emulation/MediaCudaVideoHelper.cpp b/android/emu/media/src/android/emulation/MediaCudaVideoHelper.cpp
index d2bfe8932..bd68a677f 100644
--- a/android/emu/media/src/android/emulation/MediaCudaVideoHelper.cpp
+++ b/android/emu/media/src/android/emulation/MediaCudaVideoHelper.cpp
@@ -67,7 +67,24 @@ MediaCudaVideoHelper::~MediaCudaVideoHelper() {
 void MediaCudaVideoHelper::deInit() {
     CUDA_DPRINT("deInit calling");
 
-    mSavedDecodedFrames.clear();
+    {
+        std::lock_guard<std::mutex> g(mFrameLock);
+        // Frames that were decoded but never fetched by the guest still hold
+        // a pair of textures taken from the pool. Nothing else references
+        // them, so dropping the frames would leak the textures until the
+        // whole GL share group goes away. Hand them back so the pool frees
+        // them in its next cleanUpTextures().
+        if (mTexturePool != nullptr) {
+            for (const auto& frame : mSavedDecodedFrames) {
+                if (frame.texture.size() >= 2 && frame.texture[0] > 0 &&
+                    frame.texture[1] > 0) {
+                    mTexturePool->putTextureFrame(
+                            TextureFrame{frame.texture[0], frame.texture[1]});
+                }
+            }
+        }
+        mSavedDecodedFrames.clear();
+    }
     if (mCudaContext != nullptr) {
         NVDEC_API_CALL(cuCtxPushCurrent(mCudaContext));
         if (mCudaParser != nullptr) {
